          "completion_condition": "Learner views this card and continues.",
          "narration_script": "Following up on recertifications, we need to address a specific vulnerability known as 'recertification drift.' This happens when our documentation goes on autopilot, leading to failures caused by stale goals or unchanged rationales. When an auditor sees unchanged goals across multiple episodes, it signals two things: poor clinical reassessment and incredibly weak support for medical necessity. If a patient hasn’t progressed or changed in 60 days, the immediate question is: Why are skilled services still reasonable and necessary? To protect our agency and our patients, recertification content must explicitly show progress. If there isn't progress, it must document the barriers preventing that progress and show how we have revised our interventions to address them. Here is a major audit focus to burn into your memory: A very common denial pattern occurs when the overarching recertification narrative does not match the visit-level documentation. If your daily notes show a patient struggling, but your recertification summary says they are doing fine and making steady progress, that contradiction can cost us the claim. The story must align top to bottom.",
