          "display_title": "Signature Timing and Dating Rules",
          "learner_facing_content": "Required signatures must be completed and dated according to CL-CP-001 and payer expectations.\nLate signatures are objective defects and common denial drivers in post-payment review.\nA billing hard-stop should prevent release when signature prerequisites are incomplete.",
          "learning_goal": "Apply signature timing controls to prevent avoidable billing defects.",
          "why_it_matters": [
            "Sustains documentation standards and defends care outcomes."
          ],
          "key_terms": [],
          "completion_condition": "Learner views this card and continues.",
          "narration_script": "Now let's tackle the administrative side of orders: Signature Timing and Dating Rules. While this might feel like pure paperwork, applying strict signature timing controls is one of the easiest ways to prevent avoidable billing defects. The rule is simple but unforgiving: required signatures must be completed and dated according to CL-CP-001 and the payer's strict expectations, before we bill. Late signatures are not subjective errors; they are objective defects. In post-payment reviews, an auditor doesn't have to argue clinical necessity if the signature is late—they can just deny the claim outright based on the date alone. It is consistently one of the highest drivers of denials nationwide. To combat this, we rely on a billing hard-stop. Our systems and workflows must be designed to absolutely prevent the release of a claim when signature prerequisites are incomplete. We cannot treat signatures as an afterthought or something we can 'catch up on' later. If it isn't signed and dated on time, the chart doesn't move forward.",
          "transcript_text": "Now let's tackle the administrative side of orders: Signature Timing and Dating Rules. While this might feel like pure paperwork, applying strict signature timing controls is one of the easiest ways to prevent avoidable billing defects. The rule is simple but unforgiving: required signatures must be completed and dated according to CL-CP-001 and the payer's strict expectations, before we bill. Late signatures are not subjective errors; they are objective defects. In post-payment reviews, an auditor doesn't have to argue clinical necessity if the signature is late—they can just deny the claim outright based on the date alone. It is consistently one of the highest drivers of denials nationwide. To combat this, we rely on a billing hard-stop. Our systems and workflows must be designed to absolutely prevent the release of a claim when signature prerequisites are incomplete. We cannot treat signatures as an afterthought or something we can 'catch up on' later. If it isn't signed and dated on time, the chart doesn't move forward.",
