          "display_title": "Acceptable Signature Methods",
          "learner_facing_content": "Electronic signatures must maintain identity integrity and audit traceability.\nSignature method must be consistently documented across compliance artifacts.\nAuthentication controls should be validated during onboarding and periodic audits.",
          "learning_goal": "Distinguish acceptable signature approaches and documentation expectations.",
          "why_it_matters": [
            "Sustains documentation standards and defends care outcomes."
          ],
          "key_terms": [],
          "completion_condition": "Learner views this card and continues.",
          "narration_script": "Continuing with signatures, we need to be clear on what actually constitutes an acceptable signature method. The days of only relying on wet ink are long gone, but the shift to digital comes with its own strict documentation expectations. When we use electronic signatures, we must maintain absolute identity integrity and audit traceability. An auditor must be able to see exactly who signed the document, and when. Furthermore, the method of signature must be consistently documented across all our compliance artifacts. If a physician uses a portal to sign, that digital stamp must meet all federal authentication standards. We don't just set this up once and forget it; our authentication controls should be strictly validated during physician onboarding and then double-checked during our periodic internal audits. If an auditor questions the validity of an electronic signature, we must be able to instantly produce the audit trail that proves the physician's identity. Security and traceability are just as important as the signature itself.",
