          "learner_facing_content": "<p style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\">To really cement how we document homebound status, let’s look at some defensibility examples, comparing strong patterns against weak ones.</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\">A weak documentation pattern relies on broad statements without objective support. For example, writing 'Patient is unable to safely leave home due to generalized weakness.' An auditor reading that will immediately ask: How weak? What are the symptoms? Where is the proof?</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\">Now, let's look at a strong pattern. A defensible note includes specific functional limitations, assistive needs, symptom burdens, and the context of their safety risks. A strong example would be: 'Patient requires a front-wheeled walker and the physical assist of one adult to navigate the two steps at their entryway. Leaving home requires a taxing effort due to severe COPD, which causes shortness of breath upon exertion of greater than 15 feet.'</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\">That strong example paints an undeniable picture. Finally, remember that defensible homebound documentation must be longitudinally consistent across episodes. If their condition improves or declines over a 60-day period, the specific details of their homebound status must be updated to match.</p>",
