      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "term": "Skilled Services",
          "definition": "Services requiring clinical judgment, teaching, intervention management, or assessment synthesis that cannot be safely performed by non-clinical personnel."
        },
        {
          "term": "Skilled Narrative",
          "definition": "The documented reasoning that includes assessment, intervention performed, and patient response for each visit."
        }
      ],
      "transcript": "Our final topic for this section covers Clinical Necessity, specifically identifying the Skilled Need Requirements. What actually qualifies as a 'skilled service' for coverage purposes? Skilled services are not just about performing a task; they require a clinician's unique expertise. They require active clinical judgment, specialized teaching, complex intervention management, or the synthesis of various assessment findings. A critical failure point occurs when a visit becomes 'task-only' activity without any documented skilled intent. For example, simply handing a patient their pre-poured medication is a task. Educating the patient on the side effects of a newly prescribed medication, monitoring their vitals for an adverse reaction, and titrating the dose based on physician orders—that is a skilled service. Task-only documentation will fail a necessity review almost every time. To protect the claim, your documentation must always connect the intervention you performed to active risk management and a measurable outcome. You must prove why a nurse or a therapist had to be the one to do it. Welcome back. Let's dive right into Qualifying Skilled Service Patterns. Now that we know what skilled care is in theory, how do we document it in practice? To survive an audit, we must use standardized, universally understood language for common skilled service scenarios. What are the most frequent examples of qualifying skilled services? They typically include complex wound management, monitoring an unstable medication response, and providing focused disease education where the patient or caregiver hasn't yet achieved competence. Here is the golden rule for your daily documentation: Each and every note must clearly show exactly why skilled care was required on that specific date of service. You cannot coast on the justification you wrote last week. Your skilled narrative must follow a strict three-part structure: Assessment, Intervention, and Response. First, what was your clinical assessment of the patient today? Second, what specific skilled intervention did you perform based on that assessment? And third, what was the patient's physiological or educational response to your intervention? If your note misses any of these three components, it fails to paint a complete picture of skilled care.",
      "summary": "Identify what qualifies as skilled services for coverage purposes. Use standardized language for common skilled service scenarios.",
