          "display_title": "Maintaining OASIS-POC Continuity",
          "learner_facing_content": "Use interdisciplinary review to reconcile updates in condition, goals, and interventions.\nWhen condition changes, revise POC and document rationale promptly.\nContinuity checks should occur before billing readiness confirmation.",
          "learning_goal": "Operationalize continuity checks between assessments and visit documentation.",
          "why_it_matters": [
            "Sustains documentation standards and defends care outcomes."
          ],
          "key_terms": [],
          "completion_condition": "Learner views this card and continues.",
          "narration_script": "Building on that alignment, how do we actually operationalize it? We do it by actively Maintaining OASIS-to-Plan of Care Continuity through strict checks between our assessments and our daily visit documentation. This requires a strong interdisciplinary approach. We must use interdisciplinary team reviews, or case conferences, to constantly reconcile updates in the patient's condition, their progressing goals, and our active interventions. The patient's health is dynamic, and our documentation must be, too. The rule is simple: When a patient's condition changes, you must revise the Plan of Care and document the rationale for that change promptly. You cannot wait until the end of the 60-day episode to update the plan to match what happened in week two. Finally, these continuity checks shouldn't just be clinical exercises; they must be embedded as a hard stop before billing readiness confirmation. The clinical story and the billing data must be in perfect harmony before we ask for reimbursement.",
          "transcript_text": "Building on that alignment, how do we actually operationalize it? We do it by actively Maintaining OASIS-to-Plan of Care Continuity through strict checks between our assessments and our daily visit documentation. This requires a strong interdisciplinary approach. We must use interdisciplinary team reviews, or case conferences, to constantly reconcile updates in the patient's condition, their progressing goals, and our active interventions. The patient's health is dynamic, and our documentation must be, too. The rule is simple: When a patient's condition changes, you must revise the Plan of Care and document the rationale for that change promptly. You cannot wait until the end of the 60-day episode to update the plan to match what happened in week two. Finally, these continuity checks shouldn't just be clinical exercises; they must be embedded as a hard stop before billing readiness confirmation. The clinical story and the billing data must be in perfect harmony before we ask for reimbursement.",
