      "keyTerms": [
        {
          "term": "PDGM",
          "definition": "Patient-Driven Groupings Model. The Medicare payment methodology for home health that uses diagnosis, functional status, and comorbidity data for reimbursement."
        },
        {
          "term": "Principal Diagnosis",
          "definition": "The diagnosis that reflects the dominant skilled driver for the current episode of care delivery."
        }
      ],
      "transcript": "Let's transition into the Coding Context, focusing specifically on Diagnosis and its relevance under the Patient-Driven Groupings Model, or PDGM. It is critical to understand exactly how the quality of our diagnosis coding affects our payment grouping and our coverage framing. Under PDGM, precision is everything. The principal diagnosis you select must accurately reflect the dominant, primary reason driving the need for skilled care delivery in the home. It answers the question: Why are we here most of the time? Your secondary diagnoses are just as important. They serve to contextualize the patient's overall complexity and their risk burden, which justifies the resources we are utilizing. If there is a mismatch between the primary diagnosis coded and the actual care being delivered—for example, coding a primary orthopedic issue but only sending nursing for wound care—it severely weakens our coding integrity and shatters our medical necessity support. The codes must match the care. Because diagnosis coding carries such high stakes, we must put strong Diagnosis Integrity Safeguards in place to reduce claim risk. How do we do this? First, we validate the logic of our chosen diagnoses against the actual visit content and the physician's orders before we finalize the code. We must actively avoid code selection driven by habit or history. Just because a patient had a primary diagnosis of heart failure last year does not mean it is the primary driver of their skilled need today. Our coding must reflect the patient's current, real-time clinical profile. Furthermore, if the principal diagnosis shifts across episodes—meaning the primary reason for home health changes from episode one to episode two—you must thoroughly document the clinical rationale for that shift. You cannot just quietly change the primary code without explaining to the auditor what physiological change occurred in the patient to justify the pivot.",
      "summary": "Understand how diagnosis quality affects grouping and coverage framing. Reduce diagnosis-related claim risk through validation controls.",
