          "narration_script": "Wrapping up this section, let's look at Discipline Planning and Coordination. When multiple disciplines are in the home—say, nursing, physical therapy, and occupational therapy—they must be coordinated around shared goals and, crucially, non-duplicative interventions. Every discipline's role in the home must be explicit. Their interventions must be clearly linked to their own objective outcomes. However, an auditor expects to see that these disciplines are actually talking to one another. Your coordination notes should demonstrate active communication and a tightly synchronized care plan. If an auditor sees a nurse and a physical therapist documenting the exact same generic gait-training exercises without any specialized distinction, it creates massive duplication of services. Duplicative or conflicting services are a fast track to necessity concerns and claim denials. We must work as one cohesive team, with clearly defined, complementary lanes.",
