      "transcript": "Let's move into Order Management, starting with the core rules surrounding verbal and telephone orders. In a fast-paced clinical environment, verbal orders are inevitable, but they carry significant compliance risks if they aren't captured with complete, traceable metadata. When you receive a verbal or telephone order, you cannot just jot down the clinical instruction. You must strictly document the exact order, the specific date and time it was received, the name of the receiving clinician, and the clinical context of why the order was given. Furthermore, simply documenting the order is only step one. We must track that order from the moment of intake all the way through to the practitioner's final, authenticated signature. An undocumented or unsigned verbal order is essentially an unapproved intervention in the eyes of an auditor. This brings us to a critical rule: Unresolved verbal orders must be escalated before we progress to billing. If we bill a claim with unsigned verbal orders tied to the episode, we are inviting a denial. Keep your queues clean, and track every verbal order to its finish line. Following up on verbal orders, we need to discuss Interim Order Governance. A patient's condition is rarely static, and when their condition changes, our orders must adapt. Managing these interim orders consistently is crucial for patient safety and compliance. Whenever we receive an interim order—whether it's a medication change, a new wound care protocol, or an adjusted visit frequency—it must clearly reflect the patient's current condition and be immediately integrated into the active Plan of Care. It cannot exist as an isolated sticky note in the chart; it must change the overarching care plan. For this to work, order updates must be highly visible across all our operational systems: scheduling, daily documentation, and QA workflows. If a physician changes a medication dose, the next nurse in the home needs to see that change immediately. To govern this effectively, we must manage our aging queues and set strict escalation thresholds. An interim order sitting un-actioned or unsigned for weeks is a massive liability. By keeping a tight grip on interim order governance, we reduce the risk of unresolved orders and ensure our clinicians always have the most current instructions.",
