          "completion_condition": "Learner views this card and continues.",
          "narration_script": "Following up on verbal orders, we need to discuss Interim Order Governance. A patient's condition is rarely static, and when their condition changes, our orders must adapt. Managing these interim orders consistently is crucial for patient safety and compliance. Whenever we receive an interim order—whether it's a medication change, a new wound care protocol, or an adjusted visit frequency—it must clearly reflect the patient's current condition and be immediately integrated into the active Plan of Care. It cannot exist as an isolated sticky note in the chart; it must change the overarching care plan. For this to work, order updates must be highly visible across all our operational systems: scheduling, daily documentation, and QA workflows. If a physician changes a medication dose, the next nurse in the home needs to see that change immediately. To govern this effectively, we must manage our aging queues and set strict escalation thresholds. An interim order sitting un-actioned or unsigned for weeks is a massive liability. By keeping a tight grip on interim order governance, we reduce the risk of unresolved orders and ensure our clinicians always have the most current instructions.",
