          "narration_script": "Let's transition to Survey Readiness. We are going to look at common deficiency patterns so we can proactively prevent them. The first major pattern we see during surveys is the citation for Incomplete Plans of Care. When state or federal surveyors review a CMS-485, they are looking for specific, actionable details. Incomplete plans frequently miss the mark by lacking measurable goals, omitting detailed intervention instructions, or failing to use patient-specific language. If a surveyor reads a plan and feels it could apply to any generic patient, it is incomplete. While standardized checklists are fantastic tools for improving consistency across our agency, they are not a substitute for clinical judgment. A checklist might ensure a box is ticked, but it requires clinical verification to ensure the content within that box is actually meaningful and accurate. To prevent these citations, we rely on supervisor attestations. Before a Plan of Care is released or finalized, a supervisor or QA reviewer should confirm its completeness. Catching an incomplete plan before the surveyor arrives is the ultimate goal.",
