          "display_title": "Audit Trigger: Storyline Inconsistency",
          "learner_facing_content": "Reviewer confidence depends on continuity between reason-for-care and visit execution.\nDisconnected documentation components increase denial probability.\nPerform trace checks from initial certification through final bill.",
          "learning_goal": "Sustain one clinical story across plan, visits, and claim narrative.",
          "why_it_matters": [
            "Sustains documentation standards and defends care outcomes."
          ],
          "key_terms": [],
          "completion_condition": "Learner views this card and continues.",
          "narration_script": "The second major audit trigger is Storyline Inconsistency. We touched on this earlier, but it is so vital it warrants its own focus. When an auditor reviews a chart, they are looking to sustain one unbroken clinical story across the Plan of Care, the daily visit notes, and the final claim narrative. A reviewer's confidence in our agency depends entirely on the continuity between the documented 'reason for care' and the actual 'visit execution.' If the Plan of Care says the patient needs complex wound care, but the visit notes only briefly mention the wound while spending paragraphs detailing generic hygiene assistance, the storyline is broken. Disconnected documentation components exponentially increase our denial probability. To safeguard our revenue, we must perform trace checks. We need to trace the primary diagnosis and goals from the initial certification, through the daily clinical notes, all the way to the final bill. If the thread breaks anywhere along that line, we must pause and amend the documentation.",
