          "learner_facing_content": "<p style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\">Our third Case Card focuses on Goal Quality. Establishing measurable goals is a core regulatory requirement, but it’s also the only way we can objectively prove our patient is making progress.</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\">A bad goal looks like this: 'Patient will improve mobility and get stronger.' This is a broad, well-meaning intention, but it is completely useless for care planning. How do we define 'stronger'? When will this happen? How will we know we've achieved it?</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:8px;\">A good, defensible goal looks like this: 'Patient will safely ambulate 50 feet independently using a standard walker within 14 days, to allow safe access to the bathroom.' This good example contains a specific measurable target—ambulating 50 feet. It identifies the required intervention and assistive device—independently using a walker. And it sets a strict timeframe—within 14 days. Goal quality ultimately determines whether your patient's progress can be verified objectively by an outside reviewer. If you can't measure it, you can't prove you achieved it, and you can't justify continuing care to reach it.</p>",
          "cna_practice_example": "Good: measurable target, timeframe, and intervention linkage.",
          "key_terms": [],
